    <div class="help__body">
        <section id="overview" class="card help__section">
            <h2>Overview</h2>
            <p>
                DevDb Studio creates and manages <strong>database servers as containers</strong>. Each server runs in Docker or Podman
                on this computer. Pick a database system, give it a password, and it is ready in a minute.
                Nothing but the container engine is installed on the host.
            </p>
            <div class="help__facts">
                <div><strong>Local publishing</strong><span>Every server's host port binds to <code>127.0.0.1</code>. Optional application container access also allows connections from attached application containers.</span></div>
                <div><strong>Persistent</strong><span>Data lives in a named volume and survives stop, start and restart.</span></div>
                <div><strong>Eight systems</strong><span>SQL Server, PostgreSQL, TimescaleDB, MariaDB, MySQL, MongoDB, Redis and CouchDB.</span></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="getting-started" class="card help__section">
            <h2>Getting started</h2>
            <ol class="steps">
                <li>
                    <h3>Check the setup</h3>
                    <p>Open <a href="setup">Setup</a>. It checks that a container engine (Docker or Podman) is installed and usable. The engine is detected automatically; override it on that page if you have both. On Windows the engine runs inside a WSL2 Linux distro, so Setup also checks WSL2 and lets you pick the distro. On Linux it runs directly on the host and WSL is not used. Follow the install hints for anything marked as failed, then run the check again.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <h3>Create a server</h3>
                    <p>Go to <a href="instances">Instances</a> and choose <strong>Create instance</strong>. Fill in:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Name</strong> – a label for you; it is not the database name.</li>
                        <li><strong>Database system</strong> – see <a href="help#dbms">Database systems</a> below.</li>
                        <li><strong>Host port</strong> – the port on <code>127.0.0.1</code>. A free default is suggested; each server needs its own.</li>
                        <li><strong>Password</strong> – a valid password is generated for you (the form says so); use <strong>Regenerate password</strong> for a new one, or type your own. The form shows the requirements as you type.</li>
                        <li><strong>Database name</strong> – the database created at start (not used for Redis and CouchDB).</li>
                        <li><strong>Image tag</strong> – the version to run. The default is a good choice; the first start downloads the image, which can take several minutes.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>Press <strong>Show compose preview</strong> to see the exact container settings as a compose file. You can edit the port, image tag, password and database there and the form follows; anything else is flagged as not used. Errors and warnings (duplicate name or port, weak password, floating tags, non-loopback port bindings) are listed above the editor.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <h3>Run a query</h3>
                    <p>Open <a href="query">Query</a>, choose the instance, and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Enter</kbd>. Each system has a ready-made sample query.</p>
                    <p><strong>CouchDB</strong> is queried over HTTP: write one request per line as <code>METHOD /path [JSON body]</code> (GET, PUT, POST, DELETE or HEAD), for example <code>PUT /customers</code> and <code>POST /customers {"name": "Anna"}</code>. A line that does not start with a method continues the previous JSON body, and lines starting with <code>#</code> are comments. Each reply is shown with its HTTP status; 400 and above counts as failed. The Fauxton web UI is at <code>http://127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;/_utils</code>.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <h3>Connect your own tools</h3>
                    <p>Copy the connection string from the instance card and use it in your application or database client.</p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section id="pages" class="card help__section">
            <h2>The pages</h2>
            <div class="help__grid">
                <article class="help__tile">
                    <h3><a href="instances">Instances</a></h3>
                    <p>Lists every server with its status. <strong>Start</strong>, <strong>Stop</strong> and <strong>Delete</strong> it, copy the connection string (the password is masked on screen, but the copy button copies the real one), and open the Query or Design table shortcuts.</p>
                </article>
                <article class="help__tile">
                    <h3><a href="query">Query</a></h3>
                    <p>Run SQL, mongosh or redis-cli commands, or HTTP requests for CouchDB (<code>GET /_all_dbs</code>, one request per line, JSON body after the path). Pick the database from the list, view results as a table or as raw output, and download tables as <strong>CSV</strong> or <strong>JSON</strong>. The last 20 queries per instance are kept as history, and you can save named snippets. Both are stored in your browser only.</p>
                </article>
                <article class="help__tile">
                    <h3><a href="tables">Tables</a></h3>
                    <p>Design a table without writing SQL: add columns, types, keys and nullability, preview the generated statement, then create it. The SQL differs per system; DevDb Studio generates the right dialect.</p>
                </article>
                <article class="help__tile">
                    <h3><a href="data">Table data</a></h3>
                    <p>Fill a table with <strong>1 – 10,000 random rows</strong> for testing, or clear all its data after a confirmation.</p>
                </article>
                <article class="help__tile">
                    <h3><a href="setup">Setup</a></h3>
                    <p>Checks prerequisites and lets you choose the container engine (and, on Windows, the WSL distro). The choices are saved.</p>
                </article>
                <article class="help__tile">
                    <h3><a href="status">Status</a></h3>
                    <p>Live health of all servers: state, uptime, resource use, and figures read from inside each database such as connections, number of databases and size.</p>
                </article>
            </div>
            <div class="callout">
                <strong>Which pages work with which system?</strong>
                Query and Status work for all eight. Tables and Table data are for the SQL systems only; for MongoDB, Redis and CouchDB, use the Query page.
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="dbms" class="card help__section">
            <h2>Database systems</h2>
            <p class="help__lead">Each system runs from an official image. Defaults are shown below and can be changed when you create the instance.</p>
            <div class="dbms-list">
                @foreach (var d in Systems.OrderBy(s => s.Name, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
                {
                    <article class="dbms-card" id="@d.Id">
                        <header class="dbms-card__head">
                            <span class="dbms-card__logo"><img src="icons/@d.Icon" alt="" /></span>
                            <div>
                                <h3>@d.Name</h3>
                                <span class="dbms-card__kind">@d.Kind</span>
                            </div>
                        </header>
                        <p>@d.About</p>
                        <dl class="dbms-card__facts">
                            <div><dt>Image</dt><dd><code>@d.Image</code></dd></div>
                            <div><dt>Default tag</dt><dd><code>@d.Tag</code></dd></div>
                            <div><dt>Port</dt><dd><code>@d.Port</code></dd></div>
                            <div><dt>User</dt><dd><code>@d.User</code></dd></div>
                            <div><dt>Query with</dt><dd>@d.Client</dd></div>
                            <div><dt>Tables &amp; data</dt><dd>@(d.Tables ? "Yes" : "No, use Query")</dd></div>
                        </dl>
                        <div class="dbms-card__tip"><strong>Tip</strong> @((MarkupString)d.Tip)</div>
                        <div class="dbms-card__example">
                            <span>Example</span>
                            <pre>@d.Example</pre>
                        </div>
                    </article>
                }
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="connect" class="card help__section">
            <h2>Connecting from your tools</h2>
            <h3>Connecting from the host</h3>
            <p>Use <code>127.0.0.1</code> and the host port you chose. Avoid <code>localhost</code>, which can resolve to IPv6 where the container is not listening.</p>
            <table class="help__table">
                <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Address</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>SQL Server</td><td><code>127.0.0.1,&lt;port&gt;</code> (comma, not colon), user <code>sa</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>PostgreSQL, TimescaleDB</td><td><code>127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;</code>, user <code>postgres</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>MariaDB, MySQL</td><td><code>127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;</code>, user <code>root</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>MongoDB</td><td><code>mongodb://root:…@@127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;/&lt;db&gt;?authSource=admin</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Redis</td><td><code>redis://:…@@127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;/0</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>CouchDB</td><td><code>http://admin:…@@127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;/</code> (web UI: <code>/_utils</code>)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p class="muted">The connection string on the instance card is complete and correctly escaped. Prefer it over typing one by hand.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="application-container-access" class="card help__section">
            <h2>Application container access</h2>
            <p>DevDb Studio provides a container network named <code>devdb-network</code>. Attach your application containers to this network, then enable <strong>Application container access</strong> for each database they need. The option defaults to off for each instance. DevDb Studio creates or checks the DNS-enabled bridge network when needed. Host ports still bind only to <code>127.0.0.1</code>.</p>
            <p>For newly created instances, the same checkbox on the instance card enables or disables access after creation, without recreating the database or changing its data, credentials, or loopback connection. Disabling access disconnects that database from <code>devdb-network</code> and interrupts application-container connections. If your engine rejects a change while the instance is stopped, start it and try again. Existing instances are left unchanged and do not have this control.</p>
            <p>Each new instance also has a private bridge network, <code>&lt;container-name&gt;-network</code>, which stays attached whether application container access is enabled or not. This avoids Podman's <code>pasta</code> limitation and keeps host publishing working when access is disabled. The private network is removed when the instance is deleted; <code>devdb-network</code> is retained.</p>
            <h3>Configure your application containers</h3>
            <p>Join application services to the external network in each Compose project, alongside their normal default network:</p>
            <pre>services:
  app:
    image: your-application-image
    networks:
      - default
      - devdb-network
networks:
  default: {}
  devdb-network:
    external: true
    name: devdb-network</pre>
            <p>Enable application container access for a database first so the network exists before starting Compose. Both applications and databases must use the same engine, user and WSL distro where applicable.</p>
            <p>For Docker, this feature requires <strong>Docker CLI and Engine 25.0 or newer</strong> with <strong>API 1.44 or newer</strong>. Setup checks compatibility, and unsupported versions are blocked before application network setup or enabling access. Upgrade Docker or Docker Desktop and remove any <code>DOCKER_API_VERSION</code> override below 1.44, then rerun Setup. Loopback-only instances and disabling access remain available. This Docker version requirement does not apply to Podman.</p>
            <h3>Which connection string?</h3>
            <p>Copy <strong>Host connection (loopback)</strong> for host applications. When application container access is enabled, copy <strong>Application container connection (devdb-network)</strong> for application containers. It uses the database's unique container name and internal port, not <code>127.0.0.1</code> or its published host port. For example, two MariaDB instances can have host ports 3306 and 3307, but both use internal port 3306 with different container names. Each connection string has separate reveal and copy controls.</p>
            <p>This does not expose the database to your LAN. Any container attached to <code>devdb-network</code> can attempt to connect to enabled databases; database credentials are still required. Use appropriate database credentials and do not attach untrusted containers.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="data" class="card help__section">
            <h2>Data, backups and deleting</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Stop</strong> keeps everything. <strong>Start</strong> brings the server back with its data.</li>
                <li><strong>Delete</strong> removes the container. You choose whether the data volume is deleted too; keep it if you may want the data again. A kept (or leftover) volume can be deleted later on the <a href="status">Status</a> page, Volumes tab, if no container uses it.</li>
                <li>Containers belong to the engine that created them. After switching between Docker and Podman, instances from the other engine show as <em>not found</em> until you switch back.</li>
                <li>This tool is meant for development and testing. Back up anything valuable with the database's own tools.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="troubleshooting" class="card help__section">
            <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
            <dl class="faq">
                <div>
                    <dt>Creation fails with "port is already allocated"</dt>
                    <dd>Another server or program uses that port. Pick a different host port.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>The first start takes a long time</dt>
                    <dd>The image is downloaded once. SQL Server and TimescaleDB are large. Later starts take seconds.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>The password is rejected</dt>
                    <dd>Follow the requirements shown under the password field. SQL Server has the strictest rules.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Setup shows a failed check</dt>
                    <dd>Read the hint next to it, fix it, and run the check again. Choose a specific engine (and, on Windows, distro) on the Setup page if auto-detection picks the wrong one.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>An instance shows "not found"</dt>
                    <dd>It was created with another container engine (or, on Windows, another WSL distro). Switch back on the Setup page, or delete the entry.</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>The Query page shows an error in plain text (MongoDB, Redis, CouchDB)</dt>
                    <dd>These systems report errors as text. Check the command syntax and that the right database is selected.</dd>
                </div>
            </dl>
        </section>
    </div>
</div>

